### Describe the feature you'd like to request

I think the fetch's design is better in terms of error handling, since typescript cannot type errors. So catching an error is not type-safe.

> It returns a [Promise](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise) that resolves to the [Response](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response) to that request — as soon as the server responds with headers — **even if the server response is an HTTP error status.** - https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API

If I send a 400 status code because the email is already set on a registration page, I cannot easily propagate the error back to the email input field, because tRPC can only type the 200 status.

I built a similar setup to tRPC before I found it, and I think I handled the errors better. In my case the return type was a [discriminated union](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/unions-and-intersections.html#discriminating-unions) of every possible response that the backend can send, including Internal Server Error, if the client wanted to handle that case.

I think it is an anti-pattern to use nature js errors in typescript. Sometimes throwing is useful, but it's not a safe thing to do. And I would like safety.

In my case, I defined the controllers like this:
```ts
export const authByAuthenticator = withResponse(async (req) => {
  const data = AuthByAuthenticator.schema.parse(req.body)
  const authResult = await authenticatorAuthService.authByAuthenticator(
    req.user.email,
    data.token
  )
  switch (authResult.result) {
    case 'accessDenied':
      return unauthorizedError()
    case 'wrongTOTP':
      return zodValidationError([
        {
          code: 'custom',
          path: ['token'],
          message: 'Wrong OTP'
        }
      ])
    case 'success':
      await authOTPLimit.resolve(req)
      return success({
        token: authResult.token,
        message: 'Good OTP, redirecting...'
      })
  }
})
```


### Describe the solution you'd like to see

I would like to have a way to return something like this:

```ts
if(somethingElse)
  return {
    statusCode: 400,
    result: 'somethingElse' as const,
    somethingElseThatExistsOnlyThisCase: 123
    // ...
  }
 
return {
  statusCode: 200,
  result: 'success' as const,
  somethingThatExistsOnlyThisCase: 42
  // ...
}
``` 

And I would like to get this back clientside, as well when the status is not 200
```ts
if(response.result === 'success') {
   console.log(response.somethingThatExistsOnlyThisCase)
} else if(response.result === 'somethingElse') {
   // Do something else and typesafely access special fields
   console.log(response.somethingElseThatExistsOnlyThisCase)
}
```

### Describe alternate solutions

I can achieve the same thing if I use 200 for every request, but it's not the same.

### Additional information

_No response_

